"Grey Knight" <greyknight3@yahoo.com> writes:

[identifying helmets]

> If you have plentiful methods of uncursing to deal with the OA, you can
> find out by wearing them; this is unlike rings and amulets, which might
> not give any hint of what they're doing when put on.  Opposite
> Alignment is the easiest to identify, as it will autocurse and change
> your alignment (duh); Brilliance can be spotted by the simple expedient
> of watching your stats.  Assuming you've already figured out what
> non-magical helmets look like by Doug's approach above, any others that
> give no change (when you're non-blind) will be Telepathy. If you like
> (or if you haven't spotted non-magical helmets yet), you can arrange to
> have some sort of blindfold to test Telepathy more directly.

I'd say that Brilliance is the one to "give no change (when you're
non-blind)" - Telepathy can be spotted by noting that you can now see
monsters with brains through walls (or by blinding yourself if you
don't have intrinsic telepathy yet).

A +0 helm of brilliance won't affect your stats, so won't
auto-identify when worn.  You can identify it by elimination (as you
suggest above), or by noting that it is the only metallic helmet which
does not affect spell failure rates.
